																		Re: SCP in Fallout?
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/reaperdragon4" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6729072); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6729072&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607526662" alt="ReaperDragon4" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6729072)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/reaperdragon4" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6729072); return false;" >ReaperDragon4</a></span> <span class="odate time_1598064280 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">22 Aug 2020 02:44</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4753961">
									

<p>I think the Institute is as close as the franchise has come to an SCP Foundation. They're actually a lot more powerful than the developers realized and their power needs and the door at the end of the &quot;assembly line&quot; for their synths, as well as the origin of Nick Valentine, all imply that the Institute is far more powerful than is shown on screen. The containing aspect? Yeah Maxson's brotherhood has that covered, with miniguns.</p>
<p>Also I just point out that while Eldritch entities exist in Fallout, they don't seek humans out to interfere, not like the Scarlet King or something. There are also no reality benders, no memetic hazards, cognito hazard damage type things are all tech based (like the mesmotron for example). Almost everything there that the Foundation would consider anomalous and want to contain stems from technology. There's really no need for a Foundation like entity to exist, especially not with Maxson's brotherhood acting as a crew of wild eyed zealots with pitchforks, torches and miniguns blasting anything not human and law abiding with a hail of lead.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Start with this thought for an example of something tech based the Foundation would view as anomalous that the Institute can do, if you can send data, you can send a person. We know the institute can send data because it has the molecular relay and a database supposedly full of scanned sentient copies of people's brains (from which it got the mind of Nick Valentine for my favorite character from Fallout 4.) This means that if you have a powerful enough transmitter, as in the deep range transmitter you find for Paladin Danse at Arcjet systems, you can send a person as data across the solar system to an android body in the kuiper belt. That body would need no air, runs on electricity so if you have FCs waiting on site you can use those, and when the tasks needed are done the digital mind could just beam back to earth in a form of literal telecommuting.</p>
<p>Yeah now you know why the Institute not only wanted it, but why the Brotherhood wanted to destroy them. It wasn't about synths, it was about survival, because the Institute could have reached the Brotherhood's HQ in the NCR and infiltrated the Elder's council or replaced the High Elder. They could also have slowly taken over the NCR senate. Bet you didn't think you were saving the NCR or the Brotherhood when you took on that little fetch quest did you? And the Institute does know about the NCR, they have Kellog after all. They also have synth crows (mentioned by the names &quot;the watchers&quot; or &quot;crow things&quot; in exactly two sections of quest dialog) these are actually spying on Piper's house in diamond city, along with vault 111 and a few other places.</p>
<p>Those synth crows are also present on the Long 15 in Fallout New Vegas, and a few other places. This means they probably saw the Second Battle of Hoover Dam and know about the Courier, possibly about Big Mountain and the Divide, and definitely know that a rival civilization has sprung up on the west coast that could potentially claim all the land and resources and prewar technology for themselves. And they definitely don't want rivals, or they wouldn't have said &quot;to hell with it&quot; and blasted the CPG when things weren't going their way with it. So given the chance, they WILL try to take control of the NCR and the Brotherhood of Steel, if only to keep it from expanding west and overtaking their territory or to prevent them or the brotherhood from seizing technology they themselves want.</p>
<p>All of this from three quests and one companion. Yes I know. A whole novel's worth of intrigue from that little. Mind blown. You're welcome.</p>
<p>Also fuck 76, the barrier to entry is too expensive and their security is a joke. Why the fuck should I buy it, let alone try to figure out their nonsense? Why on earth would I participate in a game where a single hacker can steal my entire inventory with the push of a few buttons? I'm OBSESSIVE with my gear in Fallout 4 and Fallout New Vegas why on earth would I risk all that hard work or getting triggered like a frustrated ten year old on hard mode just so Bathesda can get away with a slapdash job?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
